# Reviewed false positives, pinned by fingerprint.
#
# A fingerprint is commit:file:rule:line, so each entry exempts exactly one
# finding in exactly one commit. It is not a path or a rule exclusion: the same
# file, the same rule, or a later commit all still fail. That narrowness is the
# point — an allowlist that silences a scanner broadly is worse than no scanner,
# because it reads as coverage.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sprint M2/S2.1 — test fixtures that looked like credentials
#
# The values were `test-secret-key-at-least-32-chars-long` and
# `test-jwt-secret-at-least-32-chars-long` in tests/conftest.py, and an
# equivalent literal in tests/unit/test_jwt.py. They were never valid anywhere:
# they exist so the suite has signing keys that meet the 32-character floor
# Settings enforces.
#
# gitleaks was right to flag them. A high-entropy string beside `SECRET_KEY =`
# is precisely what a leaked key looks like, and a scanner that stayed quiet
# about it would be no use for the real thing.
#
# The fix was to the fixtures, not the scanner: they are now deliberately
# repetitive (`not-a-secret-not-a-secret-...`), low-entropy, and read as test
# data at a glance. These three entries cover only the commit where the old
# values already landed, which cannot be edited without rewriting published
# history.
